  "hints": [
    "A global shift preserves the difference between every adjacent pair modulo 26.",
    "Use the tuple of cyclic adjacent differences as a canonical group key; all one-letter strings share the empty key.",
    "For each string compute (s[i]-s[i-1]) mod 26 and append it under that tuple.",
    "Modulo is essential at z/a wraparound, and duplicate input strings should remain duplicated in their group."
  ],
  "editorial": "Two equal-length strings share a shifting sequence exactly when all corresponding adjacent differences match modulo 26. This key is invariant under any global shift and uniquely identifies the sequence. The independent oracle tries all 26 shifts against a representative of each existing group instead of computing difference keys.",
